## Editorial findings

### 1. Release blocker: protected-text placeholders remain in the prose

Ten raw Tier-B notes occur at chapter 49 lines 99 and 169; chapter 50 lines 113, 173, 177, and 245; chapter 51 lines 21 and 323; chapter 52 line 265; and chapter 53 line 291. They replace, respectively, “They knew”; the “Nobody declares…” exchange; “Through”; “Nothing was gained inside”; “By count”; “You'll die in there” / “We've been in three times”; the three-line arm warning; the bearing “Through”; “The Compact … Yes. Part of it”; and the S20 closing exchange.

This is not cosmetic markup. The notes expose planning logic, offer alternative speakers, and tell a coordinator what to write. They interrupt the central fight, the injury aftermath, the Compact disclosure, and the movement's closing hook. Because several units are protected wording, ordinary paraphrase is not an acceptable repair. Speaker assignment and minimal staging must respect the sequence locks already cited in the notes: the tall man and older healer remain silent to the six; Oryn owns “Everybody says that now”; and the S20 alternation begins with Karis.

### 2. The evidence sequence is intellectually strong but audio-dense

Chapter 52, especially lines 117–221, asks the reader to track several physical documents and several meanings of “page” at once. The logical chain is sound: the old year is narrowed, the lamp behavior is separated from its cause, Karis moves her measured leaf away from the folded testimony, and Cael keeps a third page off the blanket. On paper, the spatial metaphor rewards attention. For a thirteen-year-old listening, “the hide,” “the clean sheet,” “six sheets,” “the notebook,” “the ruled leaf,” “a folded paper,” “two pages,” and “a third page” can momentarily blur object, document, and category.

The answer is not to simplify the discovery or collapse the two pages; their separation is the moral and epistemic center of the chapter. A very small orientation pass—reasserting which physical object sits at which end of the blanket at one or two transitions, and ensuring pronouns have unmistakable antecedents—would preserve the reasoning while lowering working-memory load.

### 3. Story and character architecture are working

The movement has a clear escalation: road competence, a site reading, reconnaissance, a fight solved by width, an evidentiary discovery, and a controlled approach to the next danger. Teague's field knowledge becomes Cael's bodily lesson; Karis's discipline stops inference from becoming fact; Vastin's history becomes useful without making him the solver; and the young one earns attachment through deeds. The best choice is Cael's refusal to test Force in chapter 53. It makes progression costly and ethical, preserves Teague's knowledge boundary, and converts a new ability into character rather than fireworks.

## Canon and continuity audit

No verified canon contradiction was found against the supplied `BOOK_MAP`, `STATE_LEDGER` through movement 7, `CANON_RULES`, `UNIVERSE_BIBLE`, and movement plan. The following checks passed:

