Flesch and grade results should be reported, not overinterpreted. The one-syllable concrete lexicon (“stick,” “line,” “road,” “stone,” “hand”) drives the estimate toward extreme ease, while the evidence logic and unnamed-role tracking create conceptual difficulty that the formula cannot see. A global vocabulary inflation to chase 6.8 would harm the Reader Standard. Any cadence repair should instead be local and syntactic after the placeholders are replaced.

