    31	The broad man had stopped. He stood over him on a trestle's leg, grey to the lips, with his jaw working; he looked at a stick that had been moved, and at the place which it had left, and for the space of three breaths Cael thought that he would say nothing, as he had said nothing to any of the six for the whole of a cold term and a road.
    32	
    33	"Aye," said the broad man.
    34	
    35	He lowered himself onto the shale. It took him some time. The pine went down upright beside the ash, a hand from it, in the same crack; and two men sat shoulder to shoulder at the end of two rows with their faces to the flame, each with one leg stuck out stiff before him, like a pair of gateposts that have been set a little too close.
    36	
    37	Neither of them said another word.
    38	
    39	Lira had her hand over her mouth. Brom was looking very hard into his bowl. On the far side of the fire Teague's boy had opened his own mouth, and the tall man's big chapped hand, which was nearest, came down flat upon his knee and stayed there until he had shut it.
    40	
    41	"Shin," said Oryn.
    42	
    43	She had come round the fire behind the two rows, with her sleeves pushed up, and she stood at her stride and a half from the board.
    44	
    45	"I want my hands in that once before you sleep on it. May I?"
    46	
    47	"Aye," said the broad man again. It appeared that, having found the word, he meant to get the use of it.
    48	
    49	The old woman was there before the healer had knelt.
    50	
    51	She did not undo her own work. She had bound that shin to its board with four turns of webbing, and she put her thin fingers into the second turn and the third, and drew them apart, a hand's breadth, as a person holds open the mouth of a sack. It left a strip of bare skin between. She held it so, and looked at nothing.
    52	
    53	Oryn's two thick hands went in under the web.
    54	
    55	Cael had seen those women look at one another at a mill, and nod across a leg in a ravine. He had not seen this. The one's hands lay inside the other's binding, round a bone, with the eyes shut and the lips moving; and the other's held the turns open about them, quite still, for as long as it takes to count a hundred. Neither spoke. A person who had come upon them so would have thought that it was one pair of hands and somebody else's sleeves.
    56	
    57	"It's holding," said Oryn. She drew her hands out. "It's as I left it. It'll carry you on a stick, at that one's pace, and it'll not carry you at any other."
    58	
    59	The old woman let the turns close, and smoothed them once with her thumb.
    60	
    61	A man had sat down on the shale at Cael's left while he watched. He had done it as he did it always, in one piece, with his forearms on his knees; but one of the forearms was in linen to the shoulder, and lay along a slat, and he had had to manage it with the other like a thing that he had been given to hold.
    62	
    63	"You kept my left," said Teague.
    64	
    65	"You asked for three breaths."
    66	
    67	"I got them." He looked at the fire. "I'll say it once, since I'd sooner you had it from me than guessed at it. I've stood at a thing with men behind me who'd done a deal more than you did yesterday. I never had the place behind my shoulder stay so full."
    68	
    69	He got up before Cael could find an answer, and went back to his own side.
    70	
    71	Cael sat on with his bowl. He had been thanked by a man from whom he had taken something a stride away, at that very timber, in those very breaths; and the man did not know it, and could not be told. He found that the bruise on his collar-bone was a good deal easier to carry.
    72	
    73	---
    74	
    75	He went round the end of the step when the bowls were done, where the fire could not see him.
    76	
    77	The shale turned a corner there, and the flat ran on behind it, narrower, for thirty yards. There was the last of the day on it. A slab as big as a cart's bed had come off the step's face in some old frost and lay leaning against it; and before the slab a rib of rock stood up out of the floor, a hand high, as square as a doorsill.
    78	
    79	He had not come to do anything. He had come to look at where it lay.
    80	
    81	It had been listening for a day and a half. It had listened at two timbers, eight yards off, and at the lip of a ravine; it had listened on the back of a bridge, to the hardest ground that it would ever be offered. It listened now. He had not been within ten feet of that slab before it knew of it, and of the rib, and of how far the one stood from the other.
    82	
    83	That was a body's length.
    84	
    85	He stood and considered a thing which had never once been used.
    86	
    87	He knew how it went. He had seen it done at twenty paces, by a root, and written it down; and he had seen it at one, at a timber, and would have that back for as long as he had anything.
    88	
    89	He set his right heel against the rib.
    90	
    91	He did it as he had seen it done, with care, shifting the boot twice. He laid his right palm flat upon the slab, with the fingers spread, and the stone was cold, and a little rough, and as dead as any stone.
    92	
    93	Then he lined himself up.
    94	
    95	It was harder than it had looked. A man had done it in three breaths with staves coming over a baulk at his head, and Cael, with nobody near him and all the dusk to do it in, found that he did not know where his own hip was. He brought the knee in over the heel. He let the shoulder down, and turned it. He was a long way off the line, and he could feel that he was, as a person feels a picture hanging crooked; and the thing that lay along him felt it too, and waited.
    96	
    97	He moved the hip a finger's breadth.
    98	
    99	It came. He had not expected to know when it came, and there was no mistaking it. Everything that he was, from a rib of rock to a slab of shale, stood in the one straight road, with every joint in its place; and the thing along him was no longer lying there. It was standing, as a prop stands when the weight has come onto it. It had both its ends.
   100	
   101	It did not ask him. It had no need to. It wanted one word, and it had the patience of a wedge.
   102	
   103	The ribs spoke.
   104	
   105	They were low on the left, the old three, which had gone in a gorge and knit by themselves under a healer's tape, since she could not shut what she could not read. They did not ache as they ached in a frost. It was one clear line of warning across all three, such as a cracked board gives when a foot is set on it.
   106	
   107	He stood quite still, with his palm on the stone, and heard them out.
   108	
   109	The road did not go up one side of him, as a rope goes up a mast. It went through the middle of him, through the cage. A captain's went from a heel to a palm, and came out at a wrist that had been his weakest place for longer than a boy had been in his crew. Cael's would not come out at any wrist, since it would never get so far.
   110	
   111	He had set down himself where the cost of such a thing was paid.
   112	
   113	He held it for three breaths, since he had seen it held for three. He had his reasons ready, and he went over them in order, as he had gone over four on a night in a cart-shed.
   114	
   115	It had never been fired. A person does not try such a thing for the first time forty yards from a camp, at dusk, on a slab that is leaning against the step under which twelve people mean to sleep. That was one. A healer had read those ribs from the outside, once, and had said that they had been leaned on and were not to be leaned on again; and she had not been asked about this. That was two.
   116	
   117	And the man whose it was sat round a corner of rock with his arm on a slat. He would have known that stance from a mile off, in any light. He had not been told.
   118	
   119	Cael took his palm from the stone.
   120	
   121	The road went out of him, joint by joint. The thing lay down along him again, and listened; and it seemed to him that it was not in the least put out. It had waited a day and a half. It would wait until there was something in front of it that needed to be done once.
   122	
   123	"You looked like him," said Lira.
   124	
   125	She was at the step's corner, with her shoulder against the rock. He did not know how long she had been there. Brom stood behind her, filling what was left of the light.
   126	
   127	"I know. That's the trouble with it."
   128	
   129	"Did it—"
   130	
   131	"No. I lined it. I never let it go." He rubbed his left side. "It'd have come back here. Not the arm. I've that to tell her, when she'll hear it."
   132	
   133	Brom had come the rest of the way round.
   134	
   135	"You said a fire," he said. "I've left one behind that corner with two men at it who can't be told; so I'll have it here. That leaf of shale, at the wet. It was the read?"
   136	
   137	"It was the read."
   138	
   139	"I thought so. I'd only wanted it said." He looked at the slab, and at a doorsill of rock. "And she says there's a thirteenth, and whose. He put his arm through a wall for us. You've come away with the trick of it, and he's sat there thanking you for a stick."
   140	
   141	"Yes."
   142	
   143	"That's two men who've stood in front of you and paid for it." Brom said it slowly, as he priced anything. "I'd not care to carry that."
   144	
   145	"I don't care to."
   146	
   147	"No. Then you'll pay it when it can be paid, and not a day sooner; I'd not tell him what's ours, to ease my own back." He put a hand as big as a spade on Cael's shoulder, the right one, for the space of a breath. "It's entered?"
   148	
   149	"As a trial. Not counted."
   150	
   151	"That'll do me."
   152	
   153	---
   154	
   155	Karis had the lid of the middle box for a table, and her lamp on it, and she was alone.
   156	
   157	She had asked for that. Twelve people lay under the step, forty yards off, about a fire that had gone to a red eye; she could hear the big one's breathing from where she sat. She had carried the box round the corner of the rock herself, with one hand, since she was not to lean upon the other. A lamp's flame stood straight in that dry air, and it gave her a circle of wood two feet across, and she did not want any more.
   158	
   159	She had the work to do first, and she did it.
   160	
   161	It was a reduction, and there was nothing in it that asked her to be clever. She had two letters, a hooked upright and a thing like a gate; and she had the old town's way of counting, which she had taken from the spines of its tally-books by pattern alone before she would let herself read a word of them. She worked it through on the lid, twice, with her left hand kept off the wood.
   162	
   163	It would not close. She had known before she began that it would not; a year that is written by its first letters is a year with a door left open in it.
   164	
   165	She looked at what she had for some time. Her hand was not quite steady, and she observed it, as she would have observed it in anybody.
   166	
   167	She wrote the limit down first, so that it should stand above everything else: *not shown to be the same year; shown to be near it.* A clerk in an office had copied two marks forward, from sheet to sheet, for longer than she could well think about, and had never known that he was copying a date. Whoever drew the first of those sheets had reckoned as the old town reckoned.
   168	
   169	She would say the figure to the nearest hundred, and she would say *or near it*. She would say it one time.
   170	
   171	She had wanted for years to be able to say one such thing with a paper under it.
   172	
   173	It came to her as she blotted the line, and she set the pencil down. Being believed had been the thing that she wanted, for all of those years, as a person wants a meal; and a man under a town had told her what it was like when it arrived. He had said it with his eyes shut, in the hearing of five people. He had said that it was the end of being able to be wrong.
   174	
   175	She had not understood him then. She had thought that he was tired.
   176	
   177	She understood him now, sitting on a rock at the far end of the country with a lamp. Six people would walk east in the morning upon a ring that she had made in a sheet of paper with her thumbnail. A captain who could not read a hide would take his own after them, because she had said three days. An old man on a stick, who had once been the person sent to see that a filing stood, had read out two slips that afternoon and had looked at her, and waited to be told what she would enter.
   178	
   179	Nobody on that shale had asked her whether she was sure.
   180	
   181	It was a great deal heavier than not being believed had been. She had not expected that, and she thought that she ought to have.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	She laid the two of them on the lid, when the reduction was done.
   186	
   187	On her left hand was the ruled leaf, which she had torn from her notebook in front of five people. It had her table on it: seven names down the side, and what each carried, and a stride. Beneath the table she had written two sentences about lamps, in ink, and the day, and her initial.
   188	
   189	On her right was the notebook itself, open where it had lain open on a blanket. It showed her own second copy of a thing that she had read aloud once. There was an empty box ruled in its fifth line, where four or five letters had been scraped away, and one upright stroke was drawn at the box's left side.
   190	
   191	There was a hand's breadth of bare wood between the two.
   192	
   193	She had put that wood there on purpose. She had put the whole of a blanket there, by daylight, and a pebble at either end.
   194	
   195	She was not a fool. She had not carried that leaf the length of a blanket because she could see no line. An old man had lifted his hand, with a clerk's sheet in it, and had begun; and she had brought her own hand down before he could come to a tenth word, since she had known where it was going. She had known it before he opened his mouth. She had known it, if she was honest, for longer than that.
   196	
   197	There was a sentence.
   198	
   199	It existed. She would not pretend to herself that it did not. A thing does not cease to be there because a person has declined to look at it; she had learned that from a floor with a hole in it.
   200	
   201	It was not long. She found that she knew exactly how long it was, which was absurd, since she had not once let it be said, even here, even to the lamp. She knew it as she had known the length of a word under her glove for three days in a gallery, when she had four of its letters and a thing that she wished it to be. That had been six signs.
   202	
   203	This was six words.
   204	
   205	She did not say them over. She sat with her right hand on her knee and her left, in its linen, laid upon the lid; and after a moment she moved the bound one, without quite meaning to, until it lay flat over the bare wood between the two pages.
   206	
   207	She had done so once before, in that gallery. She had kept her glove on a word until every sign of it had been read off the stone by somebody else's work, one at a time; and when she lifted the glove at last, the word had not been the one that she wanted. It had been a smaller and a plainer thing, and it had turned out to be worth more.
   208	
   209	That was the whole of her reason, and she went over it to see whether it would bear.
   210	
   211	A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.
   212	
   213	If she wrote six words tonight, she would have made that thing herself, out of nothing but the wish to have it. It would be a finding by morning, in five heads. One of the five would begin to draft upon it, and he had told her that afternoon that he could feel nine orders waiting. Another had sat across a fire from her in a yard and said what he wanted from the end of this line; and whatever she wrote, he would carry it into the middle of that place with him.
   214	
   215	She would not hand him a guess to stand on.
   216	
   217	She wanted to. She let herself know that as well, since it was true, and since a refusal that costs nothing is only a habit. She had stood for years on the wrong side of shut presses. She had never in her life been so near to a thing, with a pencil in reach.
   218	
   219	She took her hand off the wood.
   220	
   221	There was a clean line left at the foot of the ruled leaf, under her initial. She wrote on it, small, and then she read what she had written, to be sure that it said no more than she meant.
   222	
   223	*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*
   224	
   225	She put the leaf into the back of the notebook, and the notebook into her coat. The lamp she left burning, since there was a thing that she had still to say that night, once, to the person who would want it most. He would see the light. He would come when he had finished writing, as he always did.
   226	
   227	---
   228	
   229	Seln came in over the lip an hour after dark.
   230	
   231	Cael had been listening for him. So had a captain; Teague was on his feet at the edge of the flat before the small shape was fairly over, and the two of them met there, out of the fire's light, with Cael a stride back at a shoulder, where he had been put.
   232	
   233	"Well?"
   234	
   235	"It is a gap in a ridge. It is square because the two walls of it are rock, and stand; I think that water cut it, and I think that it was a long while ago. There is a dry bed going in." Seln's voice was as mild as it had been at a hatch under a town. "It is the only way through those hills for five miles on either hand. I went as near as a mile. I had not the light to go nearer, and I should not have gone nearer in any case."
   236	
   237	"What's in it?"
   238	
   239	"I saw no fire, and I smelled none. I saw nobody on either wall. Nothing moved in it for the whole of an hour."
   240	
   241	"That's nothing, then," said Teague.
   242	
   243	"It is nothing that I saw."
   244	
   245	He was silent for a breath, and Cael, who had heard that man say *nothing* at the end of some hundreds of watches, heard that he had not said it now.
   246	
   247	"Seven people went east from a shelf yesterday, with a day's start of us, along a path that goes nowhere else. There is one door in those hills. I lay a mile from it until dark, and I could not find so much as a heel-mark in the bed." His hands were at his sides. "I should have liked to see something. A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for."
   248	
   249	"You don't like it."
   250	
   251	"I looked at it."
   252	
   253	It was all that he would say of the place, and Teague did not press him for more. He put his thumb in his belt, the left one, and looked east, where there was a line of hills against the stars, and one notch in it.
   254	
   255	"Then you'll look at it again, at first light, from as near as you can get and come back. I'll not move a stick off this shelf till you have."
   256	
   257	Seln inclined his head, and went to find his blanket.
   258	
   259	Cael wrote the day's log with his back to the step, by what the fire gave.
   260	
   261	*Three hundred and fifth day. A shelf of shale under a step, four miles short of a gap in the hills. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-six. Oryn is five days past her cairn; Brom's week was out today, and she had her hands in a shin tonight.*
   262	
   263	*Everything on one blanket. Karis has a finding of her own, about how a sigil goes out, and it lies on its own page. She would not have anything laid across. Vastin said the word a third time.*
   264	
   265	*Two slips under the knot of the oldest row, with two seals. He knows both, and has not named either.*
   266	
   267	*The broad man said one word to us.*
   268	
   269	*The thirteenth: lined, on a slab, behind the step. Not let go. The ribs would take it. A trial; not counted. Thirteen. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
   270	
   271	*Seln has looked at the gap. He will look again.*
   272	
   273	There was a lamp burning round the corner of the rock.
   274	
   275	He had seen it for an hour, a thread of yellow along the shale, and had left it alone; he knew what she was doing there, and that she would not thank him for a shadow across it. But the thread had not moved for some time. He shut the book, and got up, and went round.
   276	
   277	She was sitting on a stone with the box before her. The hide lay across its lid, under the lamp, with the clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge; and she had turned the whole so that the pencil line ran away from her toward the dark.
   278	
   279	"I have it," she said. "As near as it can be had. Come round to this side."
   280	
   281	He came round, and stood at her shoulder, and looked down at a calfskin with four rings on it. He had walked to every one of the four.
   282	
   283	Beyond the last of them, on the bare paper, there was a mark that he could see only because the lamp stood low. It was a ring pressed with a thumbnail, with a grain of sand at its heart.
   284	
   285	She put her forefinger down beside it.
   286	
   287	"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
   288	
   289	"And it's on the line," said Cael.
   290	
   291	"It's the *end* of the line. Oldest-last. Everything before it was practice, or maintenance, or — I don't have the word. This is the one that's been quiet since before quiet had a name."
   292	
   293	She took her finger from the paper, and looked up at him.
   294	
   295	"You still want to stand in the middle of it."
   296	
   297	"More than I've wanted anything since I was fourteen."
   298	
   299	"Why."
   300	
   301	He found that he knew, and he told her.
   302	
   303	"Because every other site, my Path worked while everyone's died. I want to know what the *oldest* one does to me. I think it's going to tell me something the records can't."
   304	
   305	He was right. He did not yet know how right.

