    95	Nobody asked him what part. He did not say. He had been forty years in the thing and had two seals in his head that he could have named, and he did not name them, and Cael watched him not do it and understood that it was not discretion. It was that the man did not yet know what he would do with the names if he said them, and had learned, in a fortnight, at a table and a counter and a cut, not to say a thing he did not know what to do with.
    96	
    97	"Six years," Karis said. She had the deep-layer sheet under her hand, the one from the spring with the office's *six years* on it in the office's rounding, and she looked at it beside the directive and beside the index, and did the thing she did, which was to say what the papers said and not one word past it. "Six years back, somebody measured all of it, and graded it, and ordered the grades to be held. Somebody was administering this — the sites, the marks, all of it — recently enough to keep records, and to fight over the records, and to seal the fight. Documented as *stable*. Somebody's still writing that word down." She looked at the fire. "The Compact. That's who keeps records. That's who has a ruling hand and a seat and a seal. I'm not going to say *someone else*, because I've no sheet for someone else, and I've a whole file for them."
    98	
    99	"The Compact," Vastin said. He said it as a man says his own name in a room where it is no longer his. "Yes. Part of it."
   100	
   101	That was all that he said about the crack. He did not pry it. He put the sheet back in its row, the order side up, and closed the row, and sat back on the shale with the stick across his knees, and Cael saw him decide, as he had seen him decide at the back of a column and at a plank and at a stake, and the decision was not to decide, and it was the right one, and it was entered.
   102	
   103	---
   104	
   105	Oryn did the broad man's leg by the fire when the blanket was folded.
   106	
   107	Day one of three. She had the leg between her hands for as long as she had had it on a slope, and came out of it grey, and said, in the register, "It's knitting. It'll bear on the third day and not before, and if you put it down on the second I'll read that too," and the broad man, who had not said a word to any of the six since a marsh, said, "Aye," which was Brom's word, and Brom, at the fire's edge, looked up at it, once, and looked down, and the two of them had exchanged a syllable across two crews and would not exchange another.
   108	
   109	The older woman with the cloth bag bound it after. Oryn let her. That was the third thing between the two healers, and it was not a look and not a nod; it was one of them putting her hands in the other's work, at the other's leave, and Cael entered it as the largest thing yet, and the least said.
   110	
   111	Teague's arm she read and did not touch. "Tomorrow's a pass," she said. "I know what a pass does to a man who leads from the front of it. Lead from the front of it with the other hand." Teague said "Noted," which was the third time from him too, and Oryn said, "I'm going to have to find another word," and did not, and went to the mule.
   112	
   113	The watch had gone east. That was the last thing on the shale before the fire went down, and it was Seln's, and he said it as he said things, to the hills, from the camp's edge, with the cache beside him.
   114	
   115	"Seven went along the shelf yesterday. Seven came off it at the east end and went up, onto the ground we're on, ahead of us. I've had the count at every halt today and it's twelve and a mule and nothing behind. Nothing beside." He did not turn round. "Nothing ahead that I can see. Which is the thing about ahead."
   116	
   117	"The pass," Cael said.
   118	
   119	"The pass is a place I'd put seven people if I had seven people and knew twelve were coming. I'd not put them at a barricade again. They learned the barricade." Seln looked at the notch in the hills, black against a sky that still had some light in it, and then at the fire, which was the first time all day he had looked at anything that was not the notch. "I looked at it. That's all. I've looked at it and I don't like it and I'll look at it again in the morning from nearer, and if I like it less I'll say."
   120	
   121	That was Seln's whole report, and it was more than he usually gave, and Cael entered it under the observation he could not file, beside the feeling of being known, which he had carried since a bridge and which had not gone away on a shale step with nothing made within a day of it, and which was, he thought, the same feeling as a man on a crest saying *the pass*, from the other side.
   122	
   123	---
   124	
   125	He and Karis were the last two at the fire, over the map, because they were always the last two at a table with a map on it, and had been since a ring.
   126	
   127	She had the reconciled sheet flat on the blanket's last fold, with the four circles and the line and the fifth circle, thumbnail-sized, past the hills, and the straightedge from Denvash laid along the line because he had laid it there, and the edge touched the fifth circle as it touched the other four, without a hair of daylight, dead along, and neither of them said anything about that, because they had said everything about that there was to say at a landing in the snow.
   128	
   129	"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
   130	
   131	"And it's on the line."
   132	
   133	"It's the *end* of the line. Oldest-last. Everything before it was practice, or maintenance, or — I don't have the word. This is the one that's been quiet since before quiet had a name." She looked at him. "You still want to stand in the middle of it."
   134	
   135	"More than I've wanted anything since I was fourteen."
   136	
   137	"Why."
   138	
   139	He told her, because it was Karis, and because she had asked the way she asked a stone how far it was from another stone, which was a question that deserved a number.
   140	
   141	"Because every other site, my Path worked while everyone's died. I want to know what the *oldest* one does to me. I think it's going to tell me something the records can't."
   142	
   143	He was right. He did not yet know how right.

