editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:5:Cael had walked that ridge once before, and never so. He went a stride behind a pair of shoulders that did not look round, with the wind on his right cheek and the pale grass combed flat before it; and whatever he saw, he had been set there to say. He saw a hare, and a hawk over it, and the place where a salt cart had turned in the wet. None of those was worth a fist. He kept them, as he kept everything, and found that being asked to look was heavier work than looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:41:She had set it there herself, on the hundred and thirteenth day: a grey thing as big as a loaf, picked out of the rut, to mark the stride at which her own sigil went out. It had lain there through a siege and a morning's measuring. It was not there now. In its place the turf was whole; and Cael, coming down to it behind her, saw why. A square had been cut, with a spade, and lifted, and laid back upon the hollow where a stone had bedded itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:51:Karis looked at the square of turf for as long as it takes to count five. Then she bent, and picked another stone out of the rut, and set it down where the first had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:123:He had set the bow on the west, since the west had the longest view. He was told that a view was no use to a man who took half an hour to get there on that hip, and another half to come back; the bow went on the short walk, always, and the legs went far. He had put the captain on the south. He was asked from which of the four a voice would carry to the other three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:139:He set out seven bowls. He had laid six for a hundred nights, and taken one back; then seven, in a loft, with one that stood empty. Tonight there was a person for each, and he went along the row twice with his lips moving, like a man who distrusts a sum that has come out even.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:169:"Carters have never been in." She set her bowl down. "It's like the last step of a stair in the dark, when you thought that there was one more. Your foot goes through where it ought to be. That's all it is; the next stride's only grass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:193:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:211:She stood at the cairn's foot with the rope over her shoulder, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, as she stood everywhere. Her face was toward the path. He had seen her look so at a notch with frost in it, on the hundred and twenty-second day, with a grey jenny's breath on his neck; and that time she had clicked her tongue, and gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:227:"Four," said Brom. "Meal, and a cheese, and corn for her, and the two skins." He set the sack down. "A hundred and twenty-three. And I never named a thing. He'd the skins filled and standing in the gate, and a price on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:253:They had walked it hard. A tree as thick as Brom's leg stood in one rut, and it had put on a hand of girth; there were the pools on either side, black and still, and the silver-backed leaves coming. Five people and a healer had taken a day and a morning over that track once, in a strip a mile wide, with a hide held open and a man sent a furlong ahead to be sighted on. Twelve went along it now between noon and dusk, since eleven of them had only to follow a quiet man who knew where it came out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:259:The basin lay below them as round as a plate. The reeds in it were green at the foot now, and old straw above; and every one of them stopped at the one edge, on every side, as though a person had gone round with shears. A pale straight path crossed them. Four grey walls stood in the water at the end of it, roofless, with the evening in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:277:It was there still; no spade had been near it. The stake stood a boot's width from it, scaled in leaves of rust, with two figures under its beaten head and nothing beneath them. Cael sat down against it, since his back knew the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:307:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:11:"Twelve," he said. "And still none over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:27:Vastin put out his hand, and lifted the foot of his own stick from its crack, and set it down again a hand's breadth to the left. It made room, on the level part, for another. He did not look up while he did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:He lowered himself onto the shale. It took him some time. The pine went down upright beside the ash, a hand from it, in the same crack; and two men sat shoulder to shoulder at the end of two rows with their faces to the flame, each with one leg stuck out stiff before him, like a pair of gateposts that have been set a little too close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:55:Cael had seen those women look at one another at a mill, and nod across a leg in a ravine. He had not seen this. The one's hands lay inside the other's binding, round a bone, with the eyes shut and the lips moving; and the other's held the turns open about them, quite still, for as long as it takes to count a hundred. Neither spoke. A person who had come upon them so would have thought that it was one pair of hands and somebody else's sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:89:He set his right heel against the rib.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:105:They were low on the left, the old three, which had gone in a gorge and knit by themselves under a healer's tape, since she could not shut what she could not read. They did not ache as they ached in a frost. It was one clear line of warning across all three, such as a cracked board gives when a foot is set on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:107:He stood quite still, with his palm on the stone, and heard them out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:111:He had set down himself where the cost of such a thing was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:139:"I thought so. I'd only wanted it said." He looked at the slab, and at a doorsill of rock. "And she says there's a thirteenth, and whose. He put his arm through a wall for us. You've come away with the trick of it, and he's sat there thanking you for a stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:173:It came to her as she blotted the line, and she set the pencil down. Being believed had been the thing that she wanted, for all of those years, as a person wants a meal; and a man under a town had told her what it was like when it arrived. He had said it with his eyes shut, in the hearing of five people. He had said that it was the end of being able to be wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:225:She put the leaf into the back of the notebook, and the notebook into her coat. The lamp she left burning, since there was a thing that she had still to say that night, once, to the person who would want it most. He would see the light. He would come when he had finished writing, as he always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:247:"Seven people went east from a shelf yesterday, with a day's start of us, along a path that goes nowhere else. There is one door in those hills. I lay a mile from it until dark, and I could not find so much as a heel-mark in the bed." His hands were at his sides. "I should have liked to see something. A watch that I can count is a watch that I can plan for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:269:*The thirteenth: lined, on a slab, behind the step. Not let go. The ribs would take it. A trial; not counted. Thirteen. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:287:"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:295:"You still want to stand in the middle of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:303:"Because every other site, my Path worked while everyone's died. I want to know what the *oldest* one does to me. I think it's going to tell me something the records can't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:23:They met no cart all that day. The file went as it had been set, and he listened to it behind him, since his eyes were wanted in front. A new sound had come into it since the ford. The lean old woman's staff and the old man's stick went down together now. Neither of them had chosen that, he thought: she walked beside the pannier, and he a stride behind Brom, ten yards apart; but two pieces of ash that strike the same road all day will find the one beat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:93:"Noted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:105:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:163:"So there's no Path opened on this hill, by anybody, for any cause. I'd have said so without her leaf. Now look at them, and tell me what they're doing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:It was not like turf; the band came to him as a book comes to a thumb, in leaves: a hundred of them, laid close, each as thick as his hand. The wet lay between two, and he followed it inward, a pace, until it widened. There was a hollow there behind the face, as big as a bucket, and it was full; he could feel the weight of the water standing in it, as he had once felt the weight of a calf on one leg. It had only a crack to come out by, no wider than a straw. And the leaf that shut it in was not part of the hill at all. It had slipped, some thaw long ago, and it stood on its foot like a slate leaned against a wall, with nothing to hold it but its own edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:283:A calf came down to look at Brom while the last skin filled. It was the size of a small ox, and it set its beak against the toe of his boot, and scraped, twice. He stood through that like a boulder, until it had found nothing that it cared for, and went away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:309:"That is off a keg. It lay in the shale under the ledge, and it has not been there for a cold term; there is no scale on it." He set a finger beside it, and did not touch it. "There are boot-nails in the mud of the pan, under the beasts' treading. They are a made boot's, in rows, and I have five different heels. The newest is a week old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:325:*The read: the ledge, for six breaths. A bucket behind a loose leaf. I wanted Pressure and went without. Brom drew the leaf with both arms. The eyes, for an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:327:*Thirty-two, and that one. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:7:Both were true still. The hills went away blue behind hills, and the wind came over the grass in long grey strokes, and a hawk hung above the southern folds. And at the end of the ridge there was a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:65:It ran away on either hand with the curve of the ground, a row of small dark uprights set a pace apart, like stitches along a hem. He let his eyes go along it, as he had once, and under his breastbone a thin held note began to ask at every one. He told it no. Something else in him had begun to ask as well, lower down, in the voice of a post that has been stood in its hole: *This far?* He told that no also, and looked at the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:103:"I told you on this turf that it was a cordon, and that they had been here more than once. I had forty bars by one lamp when I said it. There is light enough tonight to see a quarter of the round." His voice was as mild as ever. "Some of that iron is bright, and a good deal of it is not; and a path has been trodden along the outside of it, the whole way, by people who walk it. Nobody wore that to the stone since the thaw, to meet twelve strangers. It was there for whoever came."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:109:"That's one with a Path," said Teague. "He's telling me that he's got it." He had not taken his eyes from the place. "Two with bows, on the top baulk; you can see the stocks. Four with staves. And him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"That's the ravine. It's three men deep where it runs beside the fence, and the walls are sheer; I'd not want to go down them without a rope." He set a row of pricks along the right-hand side of it, close together. "That's the iron. And between the two there's a shelf. It's a hundred yards long, by my eye, and a cart wide for most of that. The line's a stride inside the iron, on the far side from the shelf, as it was the last time; Lira stopped a pace above the bars, and said that she'd go no farther."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:143:"The ravine bends there. It turns in toward the middle, and cuts the fence; you can see the bars stop on its near lip and begin again on the far one. At the bend its edge comes to within a stride and a half of the iron." He made the two lines nearly touch. "That's their door. Past it there's the path that Seln saw, a pace wide, going on round the fence to the east. It's the only way off that shelf that isn't through their own timbers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:163:He had not planned it. He had been set at a shoulder to say what he saw, and the field was not his; he had said so in front of fifty people. But he had seen those hoods on a rim, with a light at the end of his own arm, and again in a cut, where he had only stood. He had seen what a captain's whole arm did to a ton of oak. And he had watched seven shapes stand at a baulk in the cold, with nowhere behind them but a gap a stride and a half wide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:165:He gave it as a term, and he put his own word on the end of it, as a person sets a thumb to a slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:167:"Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:169:"Noted," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:187:He had left the stave with the boy. It was a watchman's, shortened for a town, with a knife-cut a third of the way through its shaft. He had carried it from a stepped cut for thirteen days without once having a use for it, since he was not a person who fought with a stick. He had put it into Cael's hands behind the brow and said only that it would do for a shelf. The cord with the lead on its end was round his own waist, where it lived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:195:He had not been able to say so by one lamp, a hundred and eighty days ago. He could say it now with his eyes shut. The most of the bars were scaled as the old stake at the Stair was scaled, in leaves, like bark. He would have put five cold terms upon those, and perhaps ten. Here and there among them stood a bar with only a skin of rust on it, set since. Where one had rotted at the turf, somebody had drawn it, and driven a new one into the same hole. And every thirty or forty paces he came to one of another kind. It was thicker than its neighbours by a finger, and its head had been beaten by a heavier mallet. The scale on it had scaled again, and come away, and scaled, until the thing was as rough as a root.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:199:He could not put a figure on it. He had never been shown iron that had stood so long. He knew only that a man had come to this hillside with a mallet, a great many cold terms before the one who drove the rest, and had set a bar every thirty paces. People had come after him, time and again, to set the ones between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:The path was a pace outside the bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:207:He had seen it from the brow as a pale thread. Under his palm it was stone. Boots had gone along it until the turf was gone, and the shale beneath had been trodden to a groove a hand deep. No posting of one cold term had done that, or of five. He knew what such a path was. There was one like it round the inner wall of the house above Ostrand, where the night clerk walked with his lantern. That one had been two lifetimes in the wearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:223:It was as the boy had drawn it. The bars stopped on the near lip and began again on the far one, twelve yards off, with the dark between. He went up the lip on his stomach, away from the iron, until he found the head of the cut and could cross on rock. Then he came down the far side as slowly as he had ever done anything, and lay in the grass above the path. The gap was sixty yards below him, and the wind was in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:229:There were two at the timbers, with the long stocks laid upon the upper baulk. One of those rubbed his right hand against his thigh, over and over, as a man does whose fingers have gone dead on a string. Three sat with their backs to the hut and staves across their knees. He judged by the set of the heads that two of the three were asleep sitting, and that the third had been told to see that they did not fall over. One walked. He came down the shelf to the gap, and through it, and along the path eastward for two hundred paces, and back. He did it once in the time that a careful man takes to say a floor schedule through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:237:*He is the one with the Path,* Seln thought. *And he has been told what is on the other side of that iron. They all have. But he is the only one of the seven who has something that it would take.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:241:None of them had a face. He had known that they would not. The hoods were up, and the cloths were tied, at the darkest hour of the night, among their own people, on a hill where nobody could have seen so much as a chin. It was done as a rule is kept, without thought. He had broken a crossbow over a stone once, in a rage that he was still a little ashamed of, since he had held a man's weapon in his hands and could not have said whether its owner was tall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:253:He measured the gap with his eye, against the height of the walker going through it. It came to a stride and a half. A person could stop it with his two arms spread. He settled his chin on his wrists, where he could see every boot that went out by it, and began to wait for the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:263:*There is a post on a shelf between a ravine and the iron: a hut, a fire, kit under a hide, two timbers. Seven, hooded. Two bows, four staves, one with a Path who has shown a light. I drew it for him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:269:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:291:A hand touched his boot while the stars were still out. It belonged to Teague's boy; he was going along the fold from one sleeper to the next, as he had plainly been told to, and waking each by the foot, without a word. When Cael sat up he could see the whites of the eyes, very large.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:7:Nine stood at the shelf's mouth, as a captain had set them. Brom was in front. Teague was behind his right shoulder, with the tall man and his spear on the one hand and Cael on the other. Then came Lira, and Teague's boy, and Karis; then Vastin on his stick; and last the broad man whom that crew called the bow, with his crossbow spanned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:59:Cael had expected that he would. There were thirty yards of open shale, and two bows being bent at the end of it; any person whom he had ever fought beside would have crossed that at a sprint. The man in front of him went at a long hard walk, with his right arm carried a little away from his side. It came to Cael, somewhere about the count of five, why that was so. A person who has run thirty yards has no breath, and no stillness; and the only thing which that man owned wanted three breaths of both.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:133:*Force-adjacent. Teague. Partial. Bronze. Impulse along the line of the frame at contact: from the ground, through the man, out at the hand, and nothing to either side of the line. Needs a thing to push against — the barricade, the ground under the feet; nothing at a distance; nothing from the air. A body's length. The cost back along the same line, every time, at the place the line comes out. Engagement field: close; braced. Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Thirteenth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:137:The hoods got up off the shale one after another, and nobody ran; they drew back behind the timbers where those now lay, askew, a body's length nearer to their own fire, and stood to them a second time. One had a stock in his hands that hung in two pieces from its string. The other had his bow whole. He set his boot in the iron while Cael watched, and hauled, and laid a bolt in the groove, and brought the nose up level with a big man's chest at eight yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:173:Cael made an entry then, in his head, since there was still no hand for a book. He made it before a boot went over, and in the tense that it would have to be true in afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:189:Cael went first, since he had no sigil for a line to take; what he carried was no Path, and no such ground had ever touched it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:191:Three people on that shelf knew why. The rest saw a boy with a stick step over a bar of iron numbered thirty-four, two strides onto the turf, and turn round. Under his breastbone the thin note had asked at the bar, and the post in him had asked at the line. He had told them no. Nothing else in him so much as blinked: there were twelve things going round a still one, as they went on any ground, and a thirteenth that was not yet a minute old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:217:He set the ash over the iron first, and the stiff leg after it. Cael saw his face as he came across, and looked away from it. A man had carried something beside him for longer than any of the four in front of him had been alive, and had told a great many others what theirs was worth; and it had left him at a stride, on a hillside, without a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:Cael went on past their fire, which had a pot beside it, and past a hut with a blanket for a door. There was a long shape under a hide with stones along its edge. There was a keg. He saw each of those as he went by it, since he had been set to see, and he heard four pairs of feet behind him on the turf and a stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:239:It came across in front of him there, twelve yards of air, and the bars ended upon its lip. Between the last of them and the drop there was a gap. He had drawn it the night before as two lines that almost met. It was a stride and a half of trodden shale, and beyond it a path went away eastward along the outside of the fence, a pace wide, and worn into the stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:There was a thing set in him like a post, which he had had from an old man in a stair; and it had come up at that gap as a dog comes up at a gate. *This far?* It was a stride and a half from the iron to the drop. He had stood at the foot of such a thing once for eighty-one counts, with nothing to do but be the reason for it. He had it in him now. One word to it, and there would have been a plane across those few feet of shale from the ground to the sky, and seven people shut upon a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:301:He understood that only afterward. They had kept that fence, those seven, or others like them before them, with a path worn into the stone to show for it. They had been told one thing about what lay on the other side. And five people had stood in it now for the time that it takes to say a slip through twice, with the wind moving their coats; and one of them was a child, and one was an old man who leaned upon a stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:309:Its owner came through the gap at a run, a stride and a half from where Cael stood. He did not look at him. He went away along the path with his elbows going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:313:Seven went east along a path, and not one of them was followed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:345:The lean old woman came down next. She had been a furlong up the hill, at the ruts, with a mule; she must have set off at the cry. She came down a broken stair of shale with her board under her arm and her staff in her hand, as a goat comes down, and did not once put the staff to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:361:Neither of them spoke. The lean one took the foot in both hands, and set her own knee against the board's end, and drew. Cael looked at the far wall of the ravine for that. He heard the man's breath, and the shale under his fingers, and one small sound from the boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:367:It took her a quarter of an hour. The sun came down the ravine's wall a hand's breadth while he watched, and all that while two broad brown hands lay round a shin as still as if they had been set there, and a pair of old ones held a foot against a board. When the brown ones came away, the leg lay straight. The healer sat down where she had knelt, on the bare rock, and put her forehead on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:373:The healer had lifted her head. She was grey to the lips. They regarded one another across a broad man's leg, the one who could knit a bone and the one who had only ever been able to keep it still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:9:He had walked in along the trodden path from the east, at his own pace, with his hands empty, and he gave his report to a captain as he gave any.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:11:"Seven. I counted them through the gap, and I counted them again over the second rise, a mile on. They did not stop to speak to one another. Nobody came up that path to meet them, and nobody has been upon it since the dew but those seven and myself." He glanced once at the timbers, where they lay askew. "They have left a bow, a stave and their fire. I should say that they do not mean to come back for any of it today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:17:She was grey still, from a shin. She had come up the broken stair of rock a tread at a time, behind the blanket, and had sat down on the upper baulk with her elbows on her knees; and from there, at her stride and a half and a little more, she had looked at a hand that did not shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:21:"A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint," said Oryn. "That's not a healer's opinion. That's a fact about a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:"Noted," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:47:"What it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be," he said. "That was the second head. I can't carry a man's shin for him." He picked the pack up by its straps, and settled it on the shoulder that had been mended at a mill. "I can carry what was on his back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:53:Cael found her at the gap, a little later, looking east along the path.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:59:"Thirteenth. His. A line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:63:"At the timbers," she said. "I saw your back go still, and I thought that it was the stick on your neck." Her eyes were still on the path. "Does he know?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:123:"I've had a bone set by that one for half my life," said the bow, with his chin toward the turf wall, "and she's never once asked me anything but whether I'd finished squealing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:149:He had asked for a left side kept, with a stick, and he had been given exactly that. A stave had come at his own bent head, and it had been met. Another had come across at the boy's throat, and the boy had put a stick up on end and taken the rest on his neck, and had not given back the length of a boot. Teague had felt the place stay full behind his shoulder. A man who is setting a line can feel that, as he feels a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:345:*Twelfth—no, count it again—thirteenth. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. Thirteen things that aren't a Path, and I got three of them in one city and one bridge. Rate's climbing and I'm writing that down because Hesk taught me to note the trend. The Span's the fourth site and the Compact fenced it, years ago by the scale on the iron, and left men on it. They knew. They've always known. The bearing runs through it and keeps going, oldest-last, and the oldest one's out there in the deep edge, quiet since they built the machine. One more site. I've wanted to stand in the middle of it since Karis said* someone made this. *Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:351:He set the count beneath it, since a count was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:353:*The thirteenth came at the timbers, at a stride, from Teague. It is not used. He is not told. That is the second such debt, and it is entered beside the first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:*Nothing opened today: not on the shelf, and not over the iron. The read at the seep was the last. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:It was the trodden path that they took, round the outside of the iron. Six of them had walked down that dish to its middle the noon before, and Cael would have gone so again. But there were people in that file with something to lose at a stride, and a beast, and nobody proposed it. They went a quarter of the round with the bars at their right hand. On the far side Karis stopped the whole file for as long as it takes to lace a boot. She stood at a bar that was like every other, and looked back across the dish. From there the grey road on the bridge's back lay end-on to her, like a ruler seen along its edge; and when she turned about, the same line went out before her, over two ridges, to a square gap in the hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:19:Nobody had ruled that either. A captain with his arm in linen to the shoulder had lifted two fingers at the fence, and had set a pace with his own boots that a child could have kept. Cael, a stride behind that shoulder, understood that it had been taken from the hindmost and handed forward, man by man, without a word. There were two at the back now who went on wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:55:She did not lay all fifty-two. She took one from the Fallow, and set it at the hide's western end, beside the first ring; and she turned it about on the wool until its head pointed as a certain stone's had pointed, on a white floor, a long way behind them. Three from the Stair went down beside the second ring, each turned to its own wall. There was one from the hall, which a girl had held her by the belt to get. And beside the fourth ring, in three rows of three, she laid the nine that she had drawn in a ravine the day before, with the pencil hardly dry on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:59:Lira set them down, one after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:65:"I shall not open it. Nobody is to say it over; I asked that once. But it is a thing that was found, and it lies on this blanket with the rest." She put it down at the corner nearest to herself, folded as it was, and set a pebble upon it. "There."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:97:"The hide says where four places are, and not what any one of them is looking at. *The next* is yours, and I shall not enter it." She blew the lead off the leaf. "Going east, the next is the older, every time. You had that from an office's corners, at a table. A thing that leans at what was there before it may as well be looking back as going on. I have that they lie one way. I have not what for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:125:"Letters. Two of the twenty-three." She drew a breath. "I have stood in a gallery under a town with the old town's tally-books open before me, a ladder's height of them. When one of those clerks wanted a year at the head of a page, and was short of room, he did not write its number out. He set down the first letter of each of its words. I must have seen it on forty leaves, and I took no account of it, since I was hunting for numbers written in full."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:139:There was the prick. A woman had set it there at a ford, with a borrowed pair of dividers, on an office's figure and a carter's hide. She had ruled her line a hand's breadth past it, and had said that she would not draw a stop which the paper did not give her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:179:It came away from a hip, and its foot found the shale. The old man had taken a step toward the blanket; his right hand was rising, with a clerk's sheet still in it, and his eyes had gone from a ruled table to the folded copy under its pebble at the far corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:209:It was a third page. He knew that as surely as he knew which way a blanket lay. It belonged at neither of her two corners, and he could not have said to which of them it would lie the nearer; and that, he thought, was the reason of reasons for keeping it off the wool. A small pale woman had just carried a leaf the whole length of a blanket so that nothing should be laid across. It would not be his hand that set a third thing down in the middle.

