editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:261:*Three hundred and fifth day. A shelf of shale under a step, four miles short of a gap in the hills. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-six. Oryn is five days past her cairn; Brom's week was out today, and she had her hands in a shin tonight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:269:*The thirteenth: lined, on a slab, behind the step. Not let go. The ribs would take it. A trial; not counted. Thirteen. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:287:"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:9:It was twelve people and a mule. Six of them stood on one side of a contract, with an old man counted among them now, and a captain's four on the other; and there were two healers, whom the count of ten left out, since a healer is not sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:49:"The next. There is one to a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:187:*Two hundred and ninety-ninth day. A beck under the ridge's foot, a mile short of the ring. A hundred and twenty-seven. Vastin: twenty of thirty, and walking. The Stair's rim at noon. Karis laid the kit's cord on the dead man's figure, from the old iron to the floor: nine lengths and a piece. It is what it was. I did not touch it. A hundred and thirty-one days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:193:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:299:*Three hundredth day. The track's end, outside the stone, at the hall's marsh. A hundred and twenty-three: four at a gate, for two skins, meal, a cheese, and corn. Vastin: twenty-one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:307:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:341:*Three hundred and fourth day. The watch's post, on the shelf above the fourth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-five; he stood inside the line this morning, and it took what he carries, and gave it back. Oryn is four days past her cairn. She knit a shin in the ravine's bed and was grey for a quarter of an hour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:355:*Nothing opened today: not on the shelf, and not over the iron. The read at the seep was the last. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:175:*Nothing was gained inside.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:99:*Three hundred and first day. A dead hold on the east road, in the lee of its gable. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-two. Oryn is a day past her cairn. A cup of water a head, and the kettle.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:105:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:115:There were eleven behind him now, and two beasts' worth of load on one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:321:*Three hundred and second day. The ridge's back, in a hollow. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-three. Oryn is two days past her cairn; she went by the chock-stones and did not look up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:327:*Thirty-two, and that one. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*

exec
/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n -F -e '\"They knew,\"' -e '\"Old enough to be tired. Young enough to be sent first.\"' -e '\"Drawn?\"' -e '\"Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They' -e '\"Through,\"' -e 'Fragment thirteen' -e 'Nothing was gained inside' -e '\"By count,\"' -e '\"You' -e '\"We' -e '\"Nobody dead. Nobody inside. The way stands open.\"' -e 'A third time on that arm' -e '\"Noted,\"' -e '\"Everybody says that now,\"' -e 'They fail the way a hundred lamps' -e '\"That is against the rule which I gave.\"' -e '\"You were not given leave to object to it.\"' -e '\"That' -e '\"The Compact,\"' -e '\"Yes. Part of it.\"' -e '\"Aye\"' -e '\"Aye,\"' -e '\"I looked at it.\"' -e 'Third-tier, documented four hundred years back' editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-{47..53}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:33:"Aye," said the broad man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:47:"Aye," said the broad man again. It appeared that, having found the word, he meant to get the use of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:63:"You kept my left," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:65:"You asked for three breaths."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:123:"You looked like him," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:135:"You said a fire," he said. "I've left one behind that corner with two men at it who can't be told; so I'll have it here. That leaf of shale, at the wet. It was the read?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:143:"That's two men who've stood in front of you and paid for it." Brom said it slowly, as he priced anything. "I'd not care to carry that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:151:"That'll do me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:233:"Well?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:241:"That's nothing, then," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:249:"You don't like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:251:"I looked at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:287:"It's here," Karis said. "Third-tier, documented four hundred years back — as old as the record, as old as the Architect, as old as the machine. The first one they made, or near it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-53.md:295:"You still want to stand in the middle of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"That's the day," said Teague. "I'll not take two sticks nearer to that than this until somebody's been and looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:51:"That is how the country lies, and so everything else on this blanket will lie as it lay on the ground. I wrote on each sheet which wall it came from, and which way that wall faced. I did not know at the time what I wanted it for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:81:"You've gone white," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:93:"That is one glance, by an instrument that I cannot check, taken without being asked for," she said. "I shall enter it so, and as yours: that the rows agree with the brass, and which way. I shall not ask you to look a second time. I know what it costs you, and a second glance by the same eye is not a second witness." She wrote. "But I laid them as the walls stood before you knelt down, and I told nobody why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:107:"I do not want you on the ground. I want the oldest row read through, sheet by sheet, by somebody who knows what a form leaves out. I have had it in my hands four times, and each time I was looking for something else." She lifted the sixth tape from the flat herself, and held it up to him. "You said at a spring that you had not been through it. Lira will hand them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:157:"The lamps," said Brom. "You said *lamps*, and shut your mouth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:167:"That is what each of you has told me, in your several ways, and it is what I have had myself, at four places."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:171:"They fail the way a hundred lamps fail when one wire's cut. Not the way a hundred souls fall silent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:231:"The fifth column," said Lira. "That's the one with the one word in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:257:"The Compact," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:261:"Yes. Part of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:263:"You know whose they are," said Lira. "The two seals. You knew them both before you'd read a word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:"You'd have drafted an order," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:21:"A third time on that arm and I'll set it in a splint," said Oryn. "That's not a healer's opinion. That's a fact about a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:"Noted," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:27:"Everybody says that now," said Oryn, to the timber under her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:41:"Then I'll not spend on them, and you'll not ask me to. That's what your sort of skin buys you: it keeps the point out and takes the knock." She rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand. "You'll have a week of colours. I'd like to see them when they go green."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:47:"What it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be," he said. "That was the second head. I can't carry a man's shin for him." He picked the pack up by its straps, and settled it on the shoulder that had been mended at a mill. "I can carry what was on his back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:67:"That's two of them, then. An old man and a captain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:95:"You heard me," said the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:191:"Aye," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:243:"Well?" said Lira, from the foot of the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:271:"That is where it goes," she said. "Whatever falls on this country. I shall not drop anything into it; I do not know what is under."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:313:"That is how I should enter it," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-51.md:329:"Through," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:77:"You said an hour," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:87:"You. Here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:99:"That's remembering. I asked you to read it." The grey eyes came round. "I walked onto this in the dark with a lantern, and I'd never seen them. I had the two places inside a minute. I'll not tell you how. You'll tell me. What does a man want, who's to lie on a hill all day and shoot into a hole?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:119:"The frost showed me their two shapes, and the print of the iron, eleven times in the one place. I'd have found them without. A piece of ground's a sum, the same as a load; you put down what a man needs, and it comes out as a place." He turned his head toward the rim. "You do sums. Do me this one. I've three and myself, and that whole dish to keep. Where?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:127:"That'll do," said Teague, and looked at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:141:"That's wrong," he said. "There ought to be one over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:161:"You saw. You were on that rim."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:247:"That's a sum. Don't any of you dress it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:269:"To a boot's width," said Lira. "You could walk it with your eyes shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:273:"We'll not go down to the reeds," said Teague. "Nor will the beast. It's the track's end, on the grass, and nobody drinks what stands in that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-47.md:295:"We left it so," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:35:"That's what kills them, out here," said Teague. It was the first thing that he had said aloud since the marsh. "Nobody burned this, and nobody starved in it. The water went down a rod one dry year, and they dug. It went down another." He turned from it. "A hold can lose its beasts and come again. It can't lose that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:71:"You'd seen both gates."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:77:"I know that you did. I was waiting for it." Teague picked a stem of grass. "That's what I came over to say, and I'd sooner say it sitting. I didn't put you at my shoulder to tell me what I've missed. A man who's only told what he's missed never learns what the other pair of eyes is worth. Tell me what I've seen as well. Then I know that there's two of us looking, and which way the second one's turned."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:79:"That's a great many fists."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:89:"So they'd been told what was coming, and that they'd do well not to have looked at it." He heard how it came out. "That's a man on a bay again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:91:"That's what I made it." The grey eyes had not left the fire. "I'd not have had the bay; I never heard of him till that big one spoke at the cairn. You had him, and you had the gates, and you put the two together sitting on your hands. I'd have liked it at noon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:97:"That's a short word for a boy who writes so much," he said. He got up in one piece, without a hand to the ground, and went back across the gap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:157:"We have a leaf upon them," said Karis. She had it out, and she read from it as she would have read a measure. "They do not answer fire, or a noise, or a blow struck on their own rock. They answer fright in one of their own kind, all together and at once; and by that and the carters' word they will answer a sigil in the same fashion. It is derived. I have never seen it tested, and I am thankful."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:179:"That's what I made it," said Teague, and Cael saw him glance at the girl as he had glanced at a strip of brass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:259:"That leaf. It's loose at the foot. There's a bucket behind it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-48.md:309:"That is off a keg. It lay in the shale under the ledge, and it has not been there for a cold term; there is no scale on it." He set a finger beside it, and did not touch it. "There are boot-nails in the mud of the pan, under the beasts' treading. They are a made boot's, in rows, and I have five different heels. The newest is a week old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:27:"That he'd gone down it like a man carrying eggs, and that it was right." Her mouth went a little to one side. "He nodded at me with the whole of his head. I thought it was going to come off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:53:"You can stand," she said. "You stood an hour at a post because a healer said so, and never moved. There's days when that's all that anybody's got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:"There," he said. "That was not there before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:97:"They knew," said Karis, under her breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:99:"That iron's been in the ground years. Not one cold term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:109:"That's one with a Path," said Teague. "He's telling me that he's got it." He had not taken his eyes from the place. "Two with bows, on the top baulk; you can see the stocks. Four with staves. And him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:121:"Drawn?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"That's the ravine. It's three men deep where it runs beside the fence, and the walls are sheer; I'd not want to go down them without a rope." He set a row of pricks along the right-hand side of it, close together. "That's the iron. And between the two there's a shelf. It's a hundred yards long, by my eye, and a cart wide for most of that. The line's a stride inside the iron, on the far side from the shelf, as it was the last time; Lira stopped a pace above the bars, and said that she'd go no farther."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:143:"The ravine bends there. It turns in toward the middle, and cuts the fence; you can see the bars stop on its near lip and begin again on the far one. At the bend its edge comes to within a stride and a half of the iron." He made the two lines nearly touch. "That's their door. Past it there's the path that Seln saw, a pace wide, going on round the fence to the east. It's the only way off that shelf that isn't through their own timbers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:147:"That's the ground," he said. "Now what's on it? She's to be asked, and I'm asking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:167:"Nobody declares. Nobody dies. They're seven people on a posting. Noted?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:169:"Noted," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:173:"That's three of them now," said Brom, to nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:277:"That's the same three that I counted you for a pole. Out, home, and don't look at the end of it. Look at his hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-49.md:281:"You lost to him. Whoever's behind those timbers hasn't had a cold term of you." The grass rustled. "And you've not got to touch anybody. You've got to be where a stick's coming, three times, before it arrives. That's a smaller thing than winning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:45:"That's two," he said, to nobody, and he did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:115:"Through," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:163:"You," said Teague. He had not turned his head; he said it backward, along the file. "With the hide. I said that you'd be asked. That ground, past the iron. Can a body stand on it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:175:*Nothing was gained inside.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:"You've stood on three of them," he said. "I've stood on none. Say how."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:183:"By count," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:253:"You'll die in there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:257:"We've been in three times," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-50.md:351:"That's a shin, and it's through in one place. I can knit it, when it's been laid straight; I can't lay it straight and knit it with the one pair of hands." She did not touch him. "May I?"

