   207	He had read that day at a spring, a figure at a time, and laid it against another that he had carried since he was fourteen. He had put the sheet back. He had not written it, and he had told nobody.
   208	
   209	It was a third page. He knew that as surely as he knew which way a blanket lay. It belonged at neither of her two corners, and he could not have said to which of them it would lie the nearer; and that, he thought, was the reason of reasons for keeping it off the wool. A small pale woman had just carried a leaf the whole length of a blanket so that nothing should be laid across. It would not be his hand that set a third thing down in the middle.
   210	
   211	He kept it where it was.
   212	
   213	---
   214	
   215	"There is something pinned to this," said Vastin.
   216	
   217	He had gone back to the oldest of the flat's six taped rows. The blanket's last work that day was his. He had read the row through to its last sheet, standing, as a man reads who has been told to and means to finish. He was holding the brown tape itself now, which had bound the row, turned inside out between his finger and thumb.
   218	
   219	"I beg your pardon. I had taken it for a clerk's tag. It is two slips, folded small, and put through with a pin under the knot, where the tape would cover them." He looked at Karis. "I should like leave to draw the pin."
   220	
   221	"Draw it."
   222	
   223	He did it with his nails. Two pieces of paper came away, each no longer than a finger, and he unfolded the first.
   224	
   225	It had a wafer of red wax at its foot, no bigger than a thumbnail, with something pressed into it.
   226	
   227	Cael saw him look at that seal. It was a very short look, and it was a look of the kind that a man gives to a face in a crowd which he had not thought to meet. Then he read out what stood above it, in the voice that he kept for the reading of a form.
   228	
   229	"*Findings under the fifth column to be held. Not for onward transmission. Not for citation. Not for the seat.*"
   230	
   231	"The fifth column," said Lira. "That's the one with the one word in it."
   232	
   233	"It is the column for the anomaly. There is a word in it on every sheet of six years, and the word is *Total*." He had not lifted his eyes. "This is what held them. I was shown a flat of survey abstracts that had been received by nobody, and I spent a cold term learning that it stood in no register. I never found the order. I had it in my own hand the whole time, under a knot."
   234	
   235	He unfolded the second.
   236	
   237	Its wax was dark, nearly black, and broader; and whatever was pressed into that was not what had been pressed into the red. He looked at it for longer.
   238	
   239	"*Objection entered: the holding of a finding is not the finding's disproof. Refer to the seat.*"
   240	
   241	Nobody on that shelf moved.
   242	
   243	He stood with a slip in either hand, on one sound leg and a length of ash, and Cael watched him read the two of them again, with his eyes only. He had drafted three orders at a spring, in as many breaths, and been stopped at each. He had tried to open a door under a town with a rank. Cael waited for the mouth to open, and for a schedule to come out of it.
   244	
   245	It did not.
   246	
   247	"I held a seal for a long while," Vastin said at last. "I was the person who was sent to see that a filing stood. I told you so on the day that I came over a river; and I told you that this thing was buried deeper than anything that I had ever helped to put down." He lifted the red slip, by the width of a finger, and then the dark one. "I have believed, since a morning in a gallery, that it was all of one piece. That whatever was done was done by one hand, to one end, with nobody inside it to say a word against."
   248	
   249	He laid the two slips down on the edge of the flat, side by side.
   250	
   251	"Somebody wanted this buried. Somebody else put it in writing that it shouldn't be. That's not a monolith. That's a crack."
   252	
   253	It was Brom who asked, since he was the one who priced things.
   254	
   255	"Who's the somebody else?" he said. "I'd want to know who it is that can say no to a thing like that, and put wax on it."
   256	
   257	"The Compact," said Vastin.
   258	
   259	He heard how that sounded, on a hillside. His eyes went once more to the two wafers, the red and the dark.
   260	
   261	"Yes. Part of it."
   262	
   263	"You know whose they are," said Lira. "The two seals. You knew them both before you'd read a word."
   264	
   265	"I did."
   266	
   267	"And you're not going to say."
   268	
   269	"I am not. I told you once that I had papers which I would not show, and I asked that it be said one time, so that nobody need ask twice." He had taken up the stick again. "I ask the same for these. I can name the two. If I did, there are people on this shale who would know where to carry that row of sheets; and I should have done, in a sentence, the thing that I was told at a spring not to do."
   270	
   271	"You'd have drafted an order," said Cael.
   272	
   273	"I should have drafted nine. I can feel all of them." The grey face did not alter. "I am looking at it. I find that it is a good deal harder than drafting."
   274	
   275	Karis had the two slips in her fingers already. She folded each upon its old creases, and put the pin back through both, and through the tape, exactly where it had been. She wrote three lines in her notebook, which were the day, and where the things lay, and that they had been read aloud and understood in part.
   276	
   277	"It is entered that they are there," she said. "It is entered that there are two seals, and that they differ. I have not been told whose, and I shall not put down a guess."
   278	
   279	She laid the row back into the flat with the rest.
   280	
   281	Nobody pried at it. A crack had been found in a thing that had stood over the whole of Cael's life like a wall, by the man who had once been its warder; and five people looked at it by the last of the light, on a blanket, and let it lie where it had been found.
   185	She laid the two of them on the lid, when the reduction was done.
   186	
   187	On her left hand was the ruled leaf, which she had torn from her notebook in front of five people. It had her table on it: seven names down the side, and what each carried, and a stride. Beneath the table she had written two sentences about lamps, in ink, and the day, and her initial.
   188	
   189	On her right was the notebook itself, open where it had lain open on a blanket. It showed her own second copy of a thing that she had read aloud once. There was an empty box ruled in its fifth line, where four or five letters had been scraped away, and one upright stroke was drawn at the box's left side.
   190	
   191	There was a hand's breadth of bare wood between the two.
   192	
   193	She had put that wood there on purpose. She had put the whole of a blanket there, by daylight, and a pebble at either end.
   194	
   195	She was not a fool. She had not carried that leaf the length of a blanket because she could see no line. An old man had lifted his hand, with a clerk's sheet in it, and had begun; and she had brought her own hand down before he could come to a tenth word, since she had known where it was going. She had known it before he opened his mouth. She had known it, if she was honest, for longer than that.
   196	
   197	There was a sentence.
   198	
   199	It existed. She would not pretend to herself that it did not. A thing does not cease to be there because a person has declined to look at it; she had learned that from a floor with a hole in it.
   200	
   201	It was not long. She found that she knew exactly how long it was, which was absurd, since she had not once let it be said, even here, even to the lamp. She knew it as she had known the length of a word under her glove for three days in a gallery, when she had four of its letters and a thing that she wished it to be. That had been six signs.
   202	
   203	This was six words.
   204	
   205	She did not say them over. She sat with her right hand on her knee and her left, in its linen, laid upon the lid; and after a moment she moved the bound one, without quite meaning to, until it lay flat over the bare wood between the two pages.
   206	
   207	She had done so once before, in that gallery. She had kept her glove on a word until every sign of it had been read off the stone by somebody else's work, one at a time; and when she lifted the glove at last, the word had not been the one that she wanted. It had been a smaller and a plainer thing, and it had turned out to be worth more.
   208	
   209	That was the whole of her reason, and she went over it to see whether it would bear.
   210	
   211	A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.
   212	
   213	If she wrote six words tonight, she would have made that thing herself, out of nothing but the wish to have it. It would be a finding by morning, in five heads. One of the five would begin to draft upon it, and he had told her that afternoon that he could feel nine orders waiting. Another had sat across a fire from her in a yard and said what he wanted from the end of this line; and whatever she wrote, he would carry it into the middle of that place with him.
   214	
   215	She would not hand him a guess to stand on.
   216	
   217	She wanted to. She let herself know that as well, since it was true, and since a refusal that costs nothing is only a habit. She had stood for years on the wrong side of shut presses. She had never in her life been so near to a thing, with a pencil in reach.
   218	
   219	She took her hand off the wood.
   220	
   221	There was a clean line left at the foot of the ruled leaf, under her initial. She wrote on it, small, and then she read what she had written, to be sure that it said no more than she meant.
   222	
   223	*Second page: nothing entered. Not for want of a pencil.*
   224	
   225	She put the leaf into the back of the notebook, and the notebook into her coat. The lamp she left burning, since there was a thing that she had still to say that night, once, to the person who would want it most. He would see the light. He would come when he had finished writing, as he always did.
   226	
   227	---
   228	
   229	Seln came in over the lip an hour after dark.
   230	

